What this solution actually does: a timer-driven Windows service (interval from the encrypted service configuration, 10 s fallback at ServiceHost.cs:31; the shared host base serialises ticks with _tickLock, WindowsServiceHostBase.cs:21). Per tick, inside a DI scope, it (1) marks the service as running, (2) reads the system date, the business-day flag and the eight CALYPSO_MQ_* parameters (operating hours, broker URI, three queues, user, password), (3) skips the tick - raising a critical alert row - when the clock is outside CALYPSO_MQ_HORA_INICIO..HORA_FIN, or logs an ERROR on a non-business day, (4) opens an ActiveMQ connection and session, (5) reads every tbl_calypso_intercambio row of type FX in status INI (usp_ObtenerSolicitudesPendientesCalypso_Conector), publishes each stored message (msg_in) to the request queue and sets the row to SENT, (6) drains up to ten messages from the response queue (Calypso acknowledgements for FX) and ten from the output queue (liquidation messages), extracting ExternalRef and Status from the XML and calling usp_RegistrarRespuestaCalypso_Conector, which either turns the matching SENT row into RECV or inserts a new RECV row, and (7) closes the connection and writes the termination marker. Context read from the central SQL project: Calypso_Forex inserts the INI rows (usp_insertar_IntercambioCalypso_RegOpeForex_CALYPSO_FOREX.sql:36) and Calypso_Liquidacion consumes the RECV/LIQ rows through msg_out (usp_CLiquidacion_GetSolicitudesLiquidacion), so this service is the only writer of the response side of the exchange table.
