3. What's Already Good
• Layered split with interfaces and constructor injection for every collaborator: ICalypsoMqServicio, ICalypsoIntercambio, IParametrosOperaciones, IOperacionServicio (Aplicacion\Interfaces\*), OperacionServicio receives them through its constructor (OperacionServicio.cs:31-38) and has no static state of its own, which is what makes the five hand-written fakes possible.
• Repository writes are strict: MarcarSolicitudEnviadaAsync and RegistrarRespuestaAsync check resultado.Exito and throw an InvalidOperationException with an actionable message (CalypsoIntercambio.cs:72-75, :106-109), and every catch logs and uses throw; (:51-55, :78-82, :111-115), so stack traces survive. This is better than the shared RepositorioMaestro default that most siblings inherit (GEN-11).
• Stored procedures are transactional and loud: SET XACT_ABORT ON, BEGIN TRY/CATCH, rollback, a write to Auditoria.ErrorLog and THROW; (usp_MarcarSolicitudEnviadaCalypso_Conector.sql:40-76, usp_RegistrarRespuestaCalypso_Conector.sql:48-111) - the swallowed-error variant noted in GEN-11 is not present.
• Secure XML handling: broker payloads go through XmlHelper.TryParseXmlSeguro (CalypsoMqServicio.cs:148), a failed parse is logged and handled without an exception, and the extraction helper is null-safe (:189-193).
• Credential hygiene in logs: the broker password is never logged and the user/password branches are commented as such (OperacionServicio.cs:99-106); Conectar logs only the URI and user (CalypsoMqServicio.cs:39).
• MQ basics done right: persistent delivery mode (CalypsoMqServicio.cs:87), consumer closed and disposed in a finally (:169-176), the connection is closed in a finally at operation level (OperacionServicio.cs:225-229), a null-session guard on both operations (:79, :99), and a bounded read (10 messages, 1 s receive timeout) so a tick cannot spin forever.
• Input handling uses an enum switch instead of dictionary indexers (OperacionServicio.cs:64-107), so a missing parameter cannot raise KeyNotFoundException; duplicates are detected and logged (ConsultaCamposBDTomi.cs:58-63); required values are validated before use (:109-120); FirstOrDefault() results are null-checked (ConsultaCamposBDTomi.cs:39-40).
• No sync-over-async, no `throw ex;`, no cryptography, no hard-coded secrets, no `DateTime.Now` in first-party code; the application layer never reads the clock directly (it asks IParametrizacion).
• The Apache.NMS drivers are vendored with repository-relative paths (Infraestructura.csproj:38,42), so they avoid the GEN-1 D:\ convention that the rest of the references follow.
• A real test project exists (five MSTest tests, interface fakes, [TestInitialize] setup) - this solution is not a GEN-2 "zero tests" case.
