5. Architecture-Fit Note
Calypso_Conector is a timer-driven poll/bridge with two directions: DB-poll -> publish to one queue -> drain two queues -> DB-update, once per tick (the shared host guarantees non-overlapping ticks) inside a business-day window; it is neither a continuous listener nor a multi-step saga, although the INI -> SENT -> RECV lifecycle is saga-like and the exchange table is already acting as a hand-rolled outbox/inbox. A Windows service is a reasonable hosting model for it today and nothing in the findings argues for a re-platform. Following the sequencing in `_ORCHESTRATION.md` §6 (./_ORCHESTRATION.md): first fix the defects that can start, duplicate or lose messages (CCN-FR-1, 2, 3, then 5 and 4), then make failures visible and add tests (CCN-FR-6, 7, 8, 11), and only then evaluate whether the poll loop should become a broker-driven MessageListener on a transacted/individual-acknowledge session with an outbox publisher (which would make FR-2, FR-3 and FR-14 disappear structurally). Unknown and needed for that decision: the real tick interval and operating window, daily volumes of FX requests and liquidation messages, Calypso's de-duplication contract for ExternalRef, ActiveMQ redelivery/dead-letter configuration, the owner and DDL of tbl_calypso_intercambio, the number of instances that run, and hosting constraints.
